also {#hindU#}¦ <ab>N.</ab> of the people of Hindusthan or Bhāratavarṣa.
 The name appears to have been derived from Sindhu, the name of the celebrated
 river where the Vedic Āryans recited their Vedic mantras. In the Avesta
 {#s#} is pronounced as {#h#}; so {#saptasinDu#} was pronounced by the
 Persians as {#haptahindu#}. The Bhaviṣya-Purāṇa speaks of {#haptahindu#}.
 Here are a few references in a few Kośas and the Purāṇas:-- (1) The
 Kālikā-Purāṇa says, {#“kalinA balinA nUnamaDarmAkalite kalO . yavanErDoramAkrAntA#}
 {#hindavo vinDyamAviSan ..”#} (2) The Merutantra of the 8th century <ab>A. D.</ab>-- {#“hinduDarmapraloptAro#}
 {#jAyante cakravartinaH . hInaM ca dUzayatyeza#}

 {#hindUrityucyate priye ..”#} (3) The Rāmakośa-- {#“hindurduzwo na Bavati#}
 {#nAnAryo na vidUzakaH . sadDarmapAlako vidvAn SrOtaDarmaparAyaRaH ..”#}
 (4) The Hemantakavikośa-- {#“hindurhi nArAyaRAdidevatABaktaH”#} (5) The
 Adbhutarūpakośa. {#-“hindurhindUSca puMsi dvO duzwAnAM ca viGarzaRe .”#}
